# 7. Docker Networking Commands

Docker networking enables communication between containers and external systems.

### List networks

```bash
docker network ls
```

### Create a network

```bash
docker network create mynetwork
```

### Inspect network

```bash
docker network inspect mynetwork
```

### Run container on a custom network

```bash
docker run -d \
  --name web \
  --network mynetwork \
  nginx
```

### Connect an existing container

```bash
docker network connect mynetwork container_name
```

### Disconnect a container

```bash
docker network disconnect mynetwork container_name
```

### Remove network

```bash
docker network rm mynetwork
docker network prune
```

### Docker Network Types

| Network | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Bridge | Container communication on one Docker host |
| Host | Shares host network namespace |
| None | Disables normal container networking |
| Overlay | Supports multi-host Docker Swarm networking |
| Macvlan | Assigns containers MAC addresses on a physical network |

### Interview Questions

**Q25. What is the default Docker network?**

Bridge is the default network for standalone containers.

**Q26. How do two containers communicate?**

Attach both containers to the same user-defined bridge network. They can communicate using container names.

**Q27. What is the difference between bridge and host networking?**

Bridge provides a separate container network namespace. Host networking shares the host network namespace.

