# 14. Dockerfile Instructions

A Dockerfile is a text file containing instructions used to build Docker images.

| Instruction | Purpose |
|---|---|
| FROM | Select base image |
| WORKDIR | Set working directory |
| COPY | Copy files |
| ADD | Copy files with additional extraction and URL capabilities |
| RUN | Execute build-time commands |
| CMD | Set default command or arguments |
| ENTRYPOINT | Define main executable |
| EXPOSE | Document application port |
| ENV | Define environment variables |
| ARG | Define build-time variables |
| USER | Set runtime user |
| VOLUME | Declare mount point |
| LABEL | Add metadata |
| HEALTHCHECK | Define health checks |
| SHELL | Set shell for shell-form commands |
| STOPSIGNAL | Set container stop signal |

### Important Interview Questions

**Q45. Difference between CMD and ENTRYPOINT?**

CMD provides a default command or default arguments.

ENTRYPOINT defines the main executable that normally runs when the container starts.

**Q46. Difference between COPY and ADD?**

COPY copies files and directories.

ADD supports additional features such as automatic extraction of local TAR archives and remote URL sources.

**Q47. Difference between RUN and CMD?**

RUN executes during image building.

CMD defines the default command or arguments when a container starts.

**Q48. What is .dockerignore?**

It excludes unnecessary files from the Docker build context.

**Q49. What is a multi-stage build?**

It uses multiple build stages to separate build dependencies from the final runtime image.

