# 15. Python Flask Docker Project

## Project Structure

```text
python-docker-app/
│
├── app.py
├── requirements.txt
├── Dockerfile
└── .dockerignore
```

### app.py

```python
from flask import Flask, jsonify

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/")
def home():
    return jsonify({
        "message": "Welcome to Docker Interview Practice",
        "status": "Running"
    })

@app.route("/health")
def health():
    return jsonify({
        "status": "healthy"
    })

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(host="0.0.0.0", port=5000)
```

### requirements.txt

```text
Flask==3.1.2
```

### Dockerfile

```dockerfile
FROM python:3.12-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY app.py .

EXPOSE 5000

CMD ["python", "app.py"]
```

### .dockerignore

```text
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.git/
.env
venv/
.venv/
```

### Build Image

```bash
docker build -t python-flask:v1 .
```

### Run Container

```bash
docker run -d \
  --name flask-app \
  -p 5000:5000 \
  python-flask:v1
```

### Verify

```bash
docker ps

docker logs flask-app

curl http://localhost:5000

curl http://localhost:5000/health
```

### Interview Questions

**Q50. Why do we copy requirements.txt before app.py?**

This improves layer caching. If application code changes but dependencies remain the same, Docker can reuse the dependency installation layer.

**Q51. Why use 0.0.0.0 in Flask?**

It allows the application to listen on all container network interfaces.

**Q52. Why use python:3.12-slim?**

It provides a smaller Python runtime image than many full Python base images.

